2. Source code — MIT
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Files:
     - scripts/**/*.py               (validators, generators, watchers, UI)
     - scripts/_*.py                 (shared helper modules)
     - Makefile
     - .github/workflows/*.yml       (CI / publish workflows)

   License: MIT License
   License text: see LICENSE-CODE at the repo root.
   SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
   Every Python file under scripts/ carries a `SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT`
   header as the authoritative per-file declaration.

Why two licenses?
  - CC-BY-4.0 is the standard license for open research data / open
    documentation. It is NOT a software license (Creative Commons
    explicitly recommends against using CC licenses for code).
  - MIT is a widely-understood permissive software license with minimal
    downstream obligations. It lets tool reuse by academic, industry, and
    hobbyist users without chilling adoption.

If you copy both dataset rows and tooling into a downstream project, carry
both attributions: cite the dataset per CITATION.cff and preserve the MIT
copyright notice in the tooling source.
